DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Benjamin's "On the Concept of History" (1940, also known as the "Theses
on the Philosophy of History") is the textual core of his philosophy of
history. Written in the final months of his life under the shadow of
fascism and the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, its eighteen theses (plus
appendices) constitute a compressed, fragmentary assault on historicism,
social-democratic progressivism, and the concept of homogeneous, empty
time. The central temporal concepts—Jetztzeit (now-time), the dialectical
standstill (Stillstand), the tiger's leap (Tigersprung), the constellation
(Konstellation), and the "weak messianic power" (schwache messianische
Kraft)—have generated an enormous secondary literature that divides roughly
into three camps: (1) theological or messianic readings that foreground
Jewish mysticism and the Kabbalah (Scholem, Wohlfarth, Moses); (2)
materialist-Marxist readings that emphasize the historical materialist
framework and the critique of progress (Tiedemann, Lowy, Tomba); and (3)
hybrid or third-position readings that insist on the constitutive tension
between theology and materialism as the productive core of Benjamin's
thought (Fenves, Khatib, Deuber-Mankowsky, Desideri).

Jetztzeit designates the eruption of a qualitatively charged "now" into
the flow of chronological time—a moment in which past and present collide
in a "dialectical image," making the past legible and politically actionable.
This is opposed to the "homogeneous, empty time" of historicism, in which
events are strung together like beads on a rosary, subject to progressive
accumulation. The "weak messianic power" (Thesis II) names the claim that
each generation inherits a small, non-triumphalist capacity to redeem
suffering generations of the past—not through eschatological fulfillment
but through the revolution of recognition. The figure of the "tiger's leap
into the past" (Thesis XIV) articulates revolutionary action as a citing of
past struggles in a new context.

Recent scholarship has expanded the domain in several directions: comparative
readings of Benjamin alongside Paul (Agamben), alongside Derrida (Levine,
Sahraoui), and alongside Cohen's neo-Kantian messianism (Desideri,
Deuber-Mankowsky). There has also been renewed attention to the monadological
structure of Jetztzeit—the idea that each historical moment contains within
it the compressed totality of history—and to the spatial implications of
Benjamin's temporal concepts (Desideri 2016).

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is the textual foundation for the entire literature review:
all major debates in Benjamin scholarship (the Arcades Project, the angel
of history, dialectical images, redemption and revolution) ultimately refer
back to the compressed arguments of the Theses. Covering this domain
thoroughly provides the conceptual vocabulary on which synthesis writers
will depend for all other domains.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Tiedemann's original German-language interpretation remains among the most
precise philological readings but is less accessible than the English
secondary literature; the Lowy book remains the most sustained commentary
but lacks abstract verification. Recent work on Benjamin's influence on
decolonial theory (Ross 2024, Vaki 2024) is included in other domains.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around the core opposition (homogeneous time vs. Jetztzeit), then
treat the messianic complex (weak power, tradition of the oppressed, tiger's
leap), then address the materialist/theological debate, and finally note
recent comparative expansions.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Theological/messianic readings: 4 papers — foreground Kabbalah, Jewish mysticism, eschatology
- Materialist/Marxist readings: 3 papers — emphasize critique of progress, historical materialism
- Hybrid/tension readings: 5 papers — treat theology-materialism tension as constitutive
- Comparative readings (Paul, Derrida, Cohen): 4 papers — expand messianic concept beyond Benjamin
====================================================================
}

